When the subject compound of a rule holds a :is() or :where() whose
argument is a single compound, Ladybird’s style invalidation keys the
rule by the class, id, attribute, tag, or state that compound requires,
so the walk an ancestor’s change starts skips every element without
it. Blink does the same and more, in RuleInvalidationDataVisitor::
ExtractInvalidationSetFeaturesFromSelectorList(): It narrows a
subject’s invalidation features to those of its :is() or :where()
arguments whenever every argument has some, which covers lists of
several alternatives too, and never does so for :not(). WebKit’s
RuleFeatureSet::recursivelyCollectFeaturesFromSelector() collects the
classes inside :is() and :where() as features of the subject. And
Gecko’s SelectorDependencyCollector::visit_selector_list() visits the
rightmost compound of each argument with the outer collector, so a
class inside one becomes a dependency of the subject.
